# Procfile
#
# A real Elastic Beanstalk Procfile for the companion
# 01_InventoryServiceApplication.java Spring Boot app, deployed via EB's
# Java SE platform (a runnable JAR, not a WAR onto Tomcat).
#
# Covers Theory chapter:
#   20) Cloud Deployment for Java Applications/Theory/01 Deploying Java
#   Applications on AWS.md ("Deploying a Spring Boot JAR to Elastic
#   Beanstalk" -- EB's Java SE platform looks for exactly this file at the
#   application root to know how to start the process; without it, EB falls
#   back to running the single JAR it finds, but an explicit Procfile is the
#   documented, deterministic way to control the startup command, memory
#   flags, and any JVM system properties).
#
# IMPORTANT -- illustrative alongside a real deploy: the jar filename below
# must match whatever `mvn clean package` (see buildspec.yml in this same
# folder) actually produces -- target/inventory-service-1.0.0.jar copied to
# the EB deployment bundle root as inventory-service-1.0.0.jar.
#
# EB reads the "web" process type and runs it as the application's single
# entry point; MaxRAMPercentage is set explicitly (matching the same gotcha
# called out for ECS/Fargate in the Theory chapter -- a JVM that doesn't
# respect its environment's actual memory ceiling can be OOM-killed by the
# platform rather than managing its own heap gracefully).
web: java -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75.0 -jar inventory-service-1.0.0.jar --server.port=5000
